#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written one-line takeaways for Jeremiah 29-52."""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 29 ----
    '29:1': "Prophecy arrives as post. The congregation that needs this word is a thousand miles from the prophet.",
    '29:2': "Carpenters and smiths are listed with the queen: Babylon deported the skilled, and took the nation's hands.",
    '29:3': "The letter travels in Zedekiah's own diplomatic bag, carrying a word against Zedekiah's whole policy.",
    '29:4': "\"Whom I have caused to be carried away.\" Before any advice, God claims the deportation as his act.",
    '29:5': "Build houses, plant gardens. The first command to exiles is to stop living out of a packed bag.",
    '29:6': "Marry, and marry off your grandchildren. The instruction assumes two generations will die in Babylon.",
    '29:7': "They are told to pray for the city that burned theirs. Their welfare is tied to their captor's.",
    '29:8': "\"Your dreams which ye cause to be dreamed\" — the comforting prophecy was commissioned by its hearers.",
    '29:9': "The test of a prophet is not sincerity but sending, and God says flatly that he did not send them.",
    '29:10': "Seventy years is a lifetime. The promise is real and nobody first hearing it will see it kept.",
    '29:11': "Thoughts of peace and an expected end — spoken to people just told to settle in for seventy years.",
    '29:12': "Prayer is promised as part of the restoration rather than as the lever that starts it.",
    '29:13': "Sought with the whole heart. Half a heart had looked for him in Jerusalem and not found him there either.",
    '29:14': "\"Whither I have driven you.\" The scattering and the gathering are credited to the same hand.",
    '29:15': "Their objection is quoted before it is answered: we have prophets of our own here in Babylon.",
    '29:16': "Those left in Jerusalem are worse off than the deported. Escaping the first wave was not survival.",
    '29:17': "Vile figs that cannot be eaten. The good figs, in the earlier vision, were the ones already carried off.",
    '29:19': "\"Rising up early and sending them.\" God is pictured as the one who got up first, and kept at it.",
    '29:21': "Ahab and Zedekiah are named. False prophecy is charged to persons, not to a climate of opinion.",
    '29:22': "Their names survive only inside a curse. Babylon burned them and the exiles kept the phrase.",
    '29:23': "Adultery and lying in God's name are charged together, and the witness is the one they lied about.",
    '29:25': "Shemaiah writes letters in his own name. The difference from Jeremiah's letter is whose name is on it.",
    '29:26': "Prison and the stocks are described as the normal office: religion's provision for a man who prophesies.",
    '29:27': "From Babylon, Shemaiah writes to Jerusalem instructing the priesthood to shut the prophet up.",
    '29:28': "He quotes Jeremiah's letter accurately, as though the quotation were its own refutation.",
    '29:29': "Zephaniah reads the letter out to Jeremiah's face instead of acting on it, and that is the whole reply.",
    '29:31': "\"He caused you to trust in a lie.\" The charge is not error but what the comfort did to the hearers.",
    '29:32': "He will not see the good God does. Living on a false hope costs a man the real one.",
    # ---- Chapter 30 ----
    '30:2': "Write it in a book. The promise is put in writing because the people who need it are not born yet.",
    '30:3': "Israel and Judah together — the restoration reaches the northern tribes lost a century earlier.",
    '30:5': "A voice of trembling, and not of peace. The book of consolation opens by granting that the fear is right.",
    '30:6': "Men holding themselves like women in labour. The picture of terror is chosen to humiliate.",
    '30:7': "\"The time of Jacob's trouble, but he shall be saved out of it.\" Not saved from the day, saved through it.",
    '30:8': "The yoke Jeremiah had worn on his own neck before them is the thing God promises to break.",
    '30:9': "Release from Babylon is not independence. They are freed in order to serve the LORD and David's king.",
    '30:10': "\"Fear thou not\" is addressed to a servant who is still standing in the land of his captivity.",
    '30:11': "A full end of the nations, a measured correction for Israel. The same blow means different things.",
    '30:12': "\"Thy bruise is incurable.\" God states the case as hopeless before he says he will heal it.",
    '30:13': "None to plead the cause and no medicines. Every human remedy is cleared off the table first.",
    '30:14': "The lovers are the alliances, and they forget her. Courted nations lose interest the moment you are poor.",
    '30:15': "She cries out over a wound whose cause has already been named to her, repeatedly, for forty years.",
    '30:16': "Those who devoured her are devoured. Being God's instrument never made Babylon innocent of using the sword.",
    '30:17': "He heals her because men called her Outcast. The taunt itself is what is quoted as the reason.",
    '30:18': "The city is built again upon her own heap — restoration on the rubble, not on a cleaner site.",
    '30:19': "Thanksgiving and the voice of merrymakers are named as the produce of the rebuilt town.",
    '30:20': "Their children as aforetime. What is promised is not a golden age but ordinary life resumed.",
    '30:21': "\"Who is this that engaged his heart to approach unto me?\" Nobody comes near on his own nerve.",
    '30:22': "\"Ye shall be my people, and I will be your God.\" The whole consolation is an expansion of this line.",
    '30:23': "The whirlwind returns in the middle of the comfort. Mercy for Jacob does not suspend judgement.",
    '30:24': "\"In the latter days ye shall consider it.\" Understanding is promised later than the event itself.",
    # ---- Chapter 31 ----
    '31:1': "All the families of Israel — twelve tribes, long since broken apart, reassembled in one clause.",
    '31:2': "Grace was found in the wilderness by people left of the sword, not in the settled land afterward.",
    '31:3': "\"I have loved thee with an everlasting love.\" The drawing is the evidence; the love is the cause.",
    '31:4': "\"Again I will build thee, and thou shalt be built.\" The passive follows the active, never the reverse.",
    '31:5': "Vines planted on the hills of Samaria. Planting for a harvest you expect to eat is the shape of hope.",
    '31:6': "Ephraim's watchmen call the pilgrimage to Zion. The old schism ends in a shout from the wrong side of it.",
    '31:7': "They are told to sing for the remnant of Israel, because a remnant is all the nation there will be.",
    '31:8': "The blind, the lame and the woman in labour are in the returning column, which moves at their pace.",
    '31:9': "They come weeping and are led by water in a straight way. Repentance and comfort arrive together.",
    '31:10': "\"He that scattered Israel will gather him.\" The nations are told, because it settles who runs history.",
    '31:11': "Ransomed from a hand stronger than his own — which is the admission that he could not buy himself out.",
    '31:12': "\"They shall not sorrow any more at all.\" The promise plainly overshoots the return from Babylon.",
    '31:13': "Mourning is turned into joy, not swapped for it. The same material is worked over into something else.",
    '31:14': "Priests satiated and people satisfied out of one supply, and the supply is called God's own goodness.",
    '31:15': "Rachel refuses to be comforted, and the text lets her refusal stand before it answers her.",
    '31:16': "\"Thy work shall be rewarded.\" The weeping itself is counted as labour, and labour is paid.",
    '31:17': "Hope is located in the end rather than in the present, which is precisely why it is called hope.",
    '31:18': "\"Turn thou me, and I shall be turned.\" Even repentance is asked for as a thing God has to give.",
    '31:19': "Shame comes after the turning, not before it. He is ashamed as a man already being received.",
    '31:20': "God speaks against Ephraim and remembers him in the same breath, and calls the remembering bowels.",
    '31:21': "Set up waymarks along the road out. They are told to mark the way into exile so they can find it back.",
    '31:22': "\"A woman shall compass a man.\" The new thing is announced and deliberately left unexplained.",
    '31:23': "The restoration is measured by a blessing people will say to each other again in ordinary talk.",
    '31:24': "Husbandmen and shepherds sharing one countryside. Peace is described as two trades not at odds.",
    '31:26': "\"My sleep was sweet unto me.\" The prophet who watched the city burn records one good night.",
    '31:27': "Sowing with the seed of man and of beast. Repopulation is put in the vocabulary of farming.",
    '31:28': "The watching that plucked up is the watching that builds. God's attention never lapsed or changed kind.",
    '31:29': "The sour-grape proverb is retired — the exiles' standing complaint that they are paying their fathers' bill.",
    '31:30': "Every one shall die for his own iniquity. The comfort in that sentence is that the account is yours.",
    '31:31': "A new covenant is promised, which concedes that the old one failed at exactly the point that mattered.",
    '31:32': "\"Although I was an husband unto them.\" The covenant was broken by one party, and it was not God.",
    '31:33': "The law is not lowered, only relocated: the same commands, written where the wanting happens.",
    '31:34': "\"They shall all know me\" rests on \"I will forgive\" — the pardon comes first and produces the knowledge.",
    '31:35': "Sun, moon and sea are brought in as collateral. God pledges the running of creation against his word.",
    '31:36': "Israel's survival is tied to the ordinances of the moon: as likely to lapse as tonight's tide.",
    '31:37': "Casting Israel off is made conditional on measuring heaven, which is a way of saying it will not happen.",
    '31:38': "The rebuilt city is laid out by named towers and gates. This promise comes with surveyors.",
    '31:40': "The valley of dead bodies and ashes is made holy. The dump and the graveyard end up inside the wall.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 32 ----
    '32:1': "Dated to Zedekiah's tenth year, the year the siege closed. The date is half of what the chapter means.",
    '32:2': "The army is outside the wall and the prophet is inside the prison court. Both facts are given at once.",
    '32:3': "Zedekiah jailed him for the content of the message, and the charge sheet quotes it accurately.",
    '32:4': "\"His eyes shall behold his eyes.\" Zedekiah does meet Nebuchadnezzar, and is blinded immediately after.",
    '32:5': "\"Though ye fight with the Chaldeans, ye shall not prosper.\" The resistance is not brave, only futile.",
    '32:7': "The land deal is announced by prophecy before the cousin turns up at the gate to propose it.",
    '32:8': "\"Then I knew that this was the word of the LORD.\" The confirmation was an ordinary visit happening.",
    '32:9': "Seventeen shekels for a field in occupied country. The sum is recorded because it was actually paid.",
    '32:10': "Signed, sealed, witnessed, weighed out. The sign God asked for is conveyancing done properly.",
    '32:11': "Sealed copy and open copy both. The legal formalities are kept exactly while the city is falling.",
    '32:12': "The deed is handed over in front of the Jews sitting in the prison court. The witnesses are deliberate.",
    '32:14': "The deeds go into an earthen jar to keep many days. The filing arrangements are the prophecy.",
    '32:15': "\"Houses and fields and vineyards shall be possessed again.\" That sentence cost seventeen shekels to say.",
    '32:16': "He obeys first and prays afterward. The purchase was made before he understood what he had done.",
    '32:17': "\"There is nothing too hard for thee\" — said by a man working up to asking whether this was a mistake.",
    '32:18': "Mercy to thousands and iniquity repaid into the children's bosom, held together in one breath.",
    '32:19': "God's eyes are open upon all the ways of men, which is comfort and threat inside the same clause.",
    '32:21': "The prayer rehearses the exodus, because the man praying needs reminding that God has done this before.",
    '32:23': "\"They came in, and possessed it; but they obeyed not.\" The whole history as gift and refusal.",
    '32:24': "\"Behold, thou seest it.\" He finishes his history lesson by pointing at the siege mounds outside.",
    '32:25': "The real question is kept until last: you told me to buy a field in a city you are handing over.",
    '32:27': "\"Is there any thing too hard for me?\" God answers by quoting Jeremiah's own opening line back to him.",
    '32:28': "The first half of the answer is that the city falls exactly as feared. Comfort here is not a reprieve.",
    '32:29': "They burned incense on the roofs; the Chaldeans burn the roofs. The sentence reuses their own fire.",
    '32:30': "\"Only done evil before me from their youth.\" The indictment leaves no period to look back on fondly.",
    '32:31': "The city has provoked him from the day it was built. Not even David's Jerusalem is exempted.",
    '32:32': "Kings, princes, priests, prophets, people. The list is drawn so no class can blame another for it.",
    '32:33': "\"The back, and not the face.\" What is charged is the posture, not any argument they ever made.",
    '32:34': "The abominations were set inside the house called by his name, and that is what made it defilement.",
    '32:35': "\"Neither came it into my mind.\" Child sacrifice is disowned as something God never thought to ask for.",
    '32:36': "God repeats their own despairing sentence about the city word for word before he contradicts it.",
    '32:37': "Gathered out of the countries he drove them to. The same wrath, once spent, becomes the road home.",
    '32:39': "One heart and one way are given, and given \"for the good of them.\" Fearing God is described as a benefit.",
    '32:40': "The everlasting covenant is secured at both ends by God: he will not turn away, and neither will they.",
    '32:41': "\"With my whole heart and with my whole soul.\" God describes his own appetite for doing them good.",
    '32:42': "The good is guaranteed by the evil already delivered. He kept the threat, so he will keep the promise.",
    '32:43': "Fields bought in a land they themselves call desolate. One absurd purchase becomes the ordinary future.",
    '32:44': "Deeds, seals and witnesses again, all over the country. What one man did in prison becomes everyone's.",
    # ---- Chapter 33 ----
    '33:1': "The second word comes while he is still shut up. Nothing about his circumstances improved in between.",
    '33:2': "The one who made the thing is the one who can establish it, and the name is offered as the guarantee.",
    '33:3': "\"Call unto me, and I will answer thee\" — said to a prisoner, about things he could not reason his way to.",
    '33:4': "Houses were pulled down to plug the breach. The city dismantled itself to survive, and did not survive.",
    '33:5': "The sorties only fill the houses with dead men, and God names whose anger did the actual killing.",
    '33:6': "Health and cure, and with them peace and truth. Part of the healing is being told the truth again.",
    '33:7': "\"As at the first.\" What is promised is not an improvement on the old order but that order returned.",
    '33:8': "Cleanse and pardon: the stain lifted and the charge dropped. Neither one by itself would do.",
    '33:9': "The nations are moved to fear by God's goodness to Judah, not by his judgement on her.",
    '33:10': "He quotes their description of the desolation back at them, in full, before overturning it.",
    '33:11': "The bridegroom's voice and the mercy-endureth-for-ever psalm, promised to streets with no inhabitant.",
    '33:12': "Shepherds bedding flocks where nothing now lives. The picture of recovery is deliberately unremarkable.",
    '33:13': "Flocks passing again under the hand of him that counts them. Someone taking inventory is the sign of peace.",
    '33:14': "The promise is not new. What is promised here is the performing of an old one already on the books.",
    '33:15': "The Branch grows up unto David, and what he does with the office is judgment and righteousness.",
    '33:16': "The name given to the king in an earlier oracle is now worn by the city: The LORD our righteousness.",
    '33:17': "David shall never want a man on the throne — said as the last king of his line is about to be blinded.",
    '33:18': "Throne and priesthood are promised together, which is how the New Testament finds them joined in one man.",
    '33:20': "The covenant with day and night is offered as the test case: break the sunrise and you may break this.",
    '33:22': "Stars and sand were Abraham's promise. It is transferred here onto David's line and the Levites.",
    '33:24': "The people already say God has cast off both families. Despair is quoted here as a form of contempt.",
    '33:26': "The oath is anchored behind David in Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, where nothing recent can reach it.",
    # ---- Chapter 34 ----
    '34:1': "The word comes while every kingdom of Nebuchadnezzar's dominion is at the wall. The setting is the argument.",
    '34:2': "Jeremiah is sent to tell the king to his face that the city he is defending will be burnt.",
    '34:3': "\"Thine eyes shall behold the eyes of the king of Babylon.\" It is the personal detail that makes it unbearable.",
    '34:4': "\"Yet hear the word of the LORD.\" Even this sentence has a mercy clause, addressed to Zedekiah by name.",
    '34:5': "A proper funeral with burnt spices and a lament. That is the entire good news given to this king.",
    '34:7': "Lachish and Azekah are named as the last two fortified cities left. The defence is down to two names.",
    '34:8': "The covenant to free slaves is struck during a siege, which says something about when reform gets tried.",
    '34:9': "The ground of release is brotherhood. A Jew may not hold a Jew, whatever the law permitted with others.",
    '34:10': "\"Then they obeyed, and let them go.\" The obedience was genuine for as long as it lasted.",
    '34:11': "The Babylonians pulled back and the freed slaves were taken again. Repentance lifts with the pressure.",
    '34:13': "The release law is traced to the exodus: a people bought out of slavery may not manufacture more of it.",
    '34:14': "Every seventh year, and the fathers never once did it. The law being broken here was already dead letter.",
    '34:15': "God grants that they had done right. He does not pretend the good act never happened at all.",
    '34:16': "\"Ye turned and polluted my name.\" The reversal is worse than the neglect because a vow was spent on it.",
    '34:17': "They would not proclaim liberty, so liberty is proclaimed — to the sword, the pestilence and the famine.",
    '34:18': "They had cut a calf in two and walked between the halves, swearing to become that if they broke faith.",
    '34:19': "Princes, eunuchs, priests and people all passed between the parts. Every class signed the same oath.",
    '34:21': "The army \"which are gone up from you\" still holds the king's future. The relief was only a pause.",
    '34:22': "God says he will command the besiegers back. The withdrawal that emboldened the slaveholders was borrowed.",
    # ---- Chapter 35 ----
    '35:1': "The Rechabite scene is dated to Jehoiakim, years earlier. It sits here for contrast, not chronology.",
    '35:2': "He is told to set out wine and press it on men who do not drink. The sermon is staged as an experiment.",
    '35:4': "The test is arranged in the temple, beside the princes' chamber, where the right people will see it.",
    '35:5': "Full pots and a direct invitation. God arranges a temptation for the pleasure of having it refused.",
    '35:6': "\"Jonadab our father commanded us.\" The refusal rests on a dead man's word, not on conviction or taste.",
    '35:7': "No house, no seed, no vineyard. A whole manner of life held for two centuries on one man's instruction.",
    '35:8': "Wives, sons and daughters are all named as keeping it. That is why the rule outlived the man who made it.",
    '35:10': "\"We have dwelt in tents, and have obeyed.\" The report is flat, and claims no difficulty in the doing.",
    '35:11': "They are inside Jerusalem only because an army drove them in. Even the exception comes with its reason.",
    '35:13': "\"Will ye not receive instruction?\" The question is put to Judah after the Rechabites have answered it.",
    '35:14': "A man's command is kept for generations. God's, spoken early and repeatedly, is not kept for a season.",
    '35:15': "The prophets' whole message reduces to return and amend, with the land named as what was on offer.",
    '35:17': "The judgement is finally grounded on two failures of hearing: I have spoken, and I have called.",
    '35:18': "What is commended is obedience to a father rather than to God. God credits it to them anyway.",
    '35:19': "Jonadab is promised a man standing before God for ever — the promise elsewhere made to David and Levi.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 36 ----
    '36:2': "All the words from the days of Josiah onto one roll. Twenty years of preaching becomes an object.",
    '36:3': "\"It may be that the house of Judah will hear.\" The sentence is written down in hope of being cancelled.",
    '36:4': "Jeremiah dictates and Baruch writes. The book exists because a second man was willing to hold the pen.",
    '36:5': "\"I am shut up; I cannot go into the house of the LORD.\" Barred from the temple, he sends his voice in.",
    '36:6': "The fast day is chosen for the reading: the one day the crowd has already come in expecting bad news.",
    '36:9': "The fast falls in the ninth month, a year after the roll was written. The reading waited for its day.",
    '36:10': "Baruch reads from a borrowed chamber in the higher court. The word gets its hearing by loan of office space.",
    '36:12': "Michaiah goes straight down to the princes. The report travels further than the reader had arranged.",
    '36:14': "They send for the roll rather than for the man. It is the document the government wants in its hands.",
    '36:15': "\"Sit down now, and read it in our ears.\" The princes give the prophecy the hearing the king will not.",
    '36:16': "They were afraid, and the fear produced only a promise to pass the matter upward to the king.",
    '36:17': "\"How didst thou write all these words at his mouth?\" They ask about method, which is a way of not answering.",
    '36:18': "\"I wrote them with ink in the book.\" The answer is entirely ordinary, and that is the claim being made.",
    '36:19': "The princes' practical help is advice to disappear. They know their own king better than they will say.",
    '36:21': "Jehudi reads the same words a second time, now to the king. Identical text, opposite reception.",
    '36:22': "The ninth month, and a fire on the hearth. The means of destroying the roll is already lit in the room.",
    '36:23': "Three or four leaves, then the penknife and the flame. He destroys it a column at a time as it is read.",
    '36:24': "Nobody rent his garments. Josiah tore his clothes over a scroll; his son sat still and kept warm.",
    '36:25': "Three princes ask him not to burn it. Their names are kept, and so is the fact that he would not hear.",
    '36:26': "Warrants go out for the scribe and the prophet, and the note is simply that the LORD hid them.",
    '36:28': "\"Take thee again another roll.\" The answer to the fire is dictation, begun over from the first word.",
    '36:29': "The king's own question is quoted back at him. He burned the answer rather than dispute the writing.",
    '36:30': "His body cast out to the heat and the frost. The man who sat by a warm hearth is promised the open weather.",
    '36:31': "\"But they hearkened not.\" The account closes on the failure of hearing rather than on the burning.",
    '36:32': "The second roll has many like words added. Burning the first one cost Jehoiakim the shorter version.",
    # ---- Chapter 37 ----
    '37:2': "Neither the king, nor his servants, nor the people of the land. The deafness is spread evenly by name.",
    '37:3': "\"Pray now unto the LORD our God for us.\" Prayer is requested from the man whose word they will not take.",
    '37:5': "Pharaoh's army marches and the siege lifts. For a few weeks the false prophets looked like the accurate ones.",
    '37:7': "The army come to help shall return to Egypt. The rescue is real, and it is going home.",
    '37:8': "\"And take it, and burn it with fire.\" The reprieve changes the date and changes nothing else.",
    '37:9': "\"Deceive not yourselves.\" The lie under indictment is the one the hearers are telling themselves.",
    '37:10': "Wounded men left in their tents would rise and burn the city. The outcome is not a matter of arithmetic.",
    '37:12': "With the siege lifted he leaves for Benjamin on property business. The prophet takes the gap like anyone else.",
    '37:13': "A captain at the gate reads the errand as desertion. The man who urged surrender is easy to suspect.",
    '37:14': "\"It is false; I fall not away.\" The denial is recorded, and alters nothing about what is done to him.",
    '37:15': "They beat him and jail him in a scribe's house made over into a prison. Even the building was repurposed.",
    '37:16': "The dungeon, the cabins, and many days. The length of it is stated with no complaint attached.",
    '37:17': "\"Is there any word from the LORD?\" Asked in secret, and answered with the word already given in public.",
    '37:18': "\"What have I offended?\" He argues his case on the merits, to a king who has charged him with nothing.",
    '37:19': "\"Where are now your prophets?\" The men who promised safety are not in prison, and are not in evidence.",
    '37:20': "He does not ask for release, only not to be sent back. He knows what that house does to a man.",
    '37:21': "A daily loaf from the bakers' street, until the city's bread ran out. The mercy lasts as long as the flour.",
    # ---- Chapter 38 ----
    '38:2': "\"He shall have his life for a prey.\" Surrender is offered as plunder: the only spoil left is breath.",
    '38:4': "The charge is weakening the hands of the men of war. Telling the truth is prosecuted as damage to morale.",
    '38:5': "\"The king is not he that can do any thing against you.\" Zedekiah gives him up by pleading helplessness.",
    '38:6': "No water in the cistern, but mire. He is lowered into a place that kills slowly and leaves no marks.",
    '38:7': "An Ethiopian eunuch of the palace is the one who hears and moves. Help comes from outside every circle.",
    '38:9': "\"These men have done evil.\" A foreigner says plainly at the gate what the princes of Judah would not.",
    '38:10': "Thirty men are detailed to lift one starving prisoner. The king acts decisively in the harmless direction.",
    '38:11': "He goes to the treasury and brings out old rotten rags. What saves the prophet's life is the palace's refuse.",
    '38:12': "\"Put now these old cast clouts under thine armholes.\" The kindness is in noticing that ropes cut.",
    '38:13': "They drew him up, and he stayed in the court of the prison. The rescue returns him to custody.",
    '38:14': "\"I will ask thee a thing; hide nothing from me.\" The king wants the word without being seen to want it.",
    '38:15': "Jeremiah states the terms first: you will not listen, and answering you has already cost me once.",
    '38:16': "He swears by the God who made the soul that he will not take this soul, and he swears it secretly.",
    '38:17': "Going out would save the city as well as the man. Surrender is offered as the way to keep both.",
    '38:18': "The alternative is put without a third branch. The choice offered Zedekiah has exactly two outcomes.",
    '38:19': "\"I am afraid of the Jews that are fallen to the Chaldeans.\" A king's fear of mockery decides a city.",
    '38:20': "\"Obey, I beseech thee.\" The prophet pleads with the king as though the outcome were still open.",
    '38:22': "The palace women will chant it: thy feet are sunk in the mire. The taunt borrows the pit he was let into.",
    '38:23': "\"Thou shalt cause this city to be burned.\" Babylon will light it and the act is charged to him.",
    '38:24': "The king's last request is secrecy. He bargains over the leak instead of over the decision.",
    '38:27': "Jeremiah repeats the cover story he was handed, and the matter was not perceived.",
    '38:28': "He was in the court of the prison when the city fell. The safest place in Jerusalem had been the jail.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 39 ----
    '39:2': "\"The city was broken up.\" Eighteen months of siege come down in the record to one dated half-verse.",
    '39:4': "The king who would not go out to the Chaldeans goes out by night, through the garden gate.",
    '39:5': "Overtaken in the plains of Jericho. He got as far as the place where the taking of the land began.",
    '39:6': "They kill his sons in front of him, and that is the last thing those eyes are used for.",
    '39:7': "Both halves of the old word land at once: he saw the eyes of Babylon's king, and went to Babylon blind.",
    '39:8': "Palace and houses burn together, and the wall that the whole argument had been about is pulled down.",
    '39:9': "The deserters are deported with the defenders. Falling away early bought a place in the same column.",
    '39:10': "The poor who had nothing are left behind and given fields. The judgement ended by redistributing the land.",
    '39:11': "Nebuchadnezzar gives personal orders about one prisoner. The prophet's name reached the top of the empire.",
    '39:12': "\"Do unto him even as he shall say.\" The man Judah jailed is handed his own terms by the invader.",
    '39:14': "Taken out of the prison court and sent home, into the ruin he had spent forty years describing.",
    '39:16': "Ebedmelech is told the city falls before he is told he is spared. His rescue is set inside the disaster.",
    '39:17': "\"Thou shalt not be given into the hand of the men of whom thou art afraid.\" The fear is granted, then met.",
    '39:18': "\"Because thou hast put thy trust in me.\" Old rags and a rope are read back to him as faith.",
    # ---- Chapter 40 ----
    '40:2': "\"The LORD thy God hath pronounced this evil upon this place.\" A Babylonian officer preaches the sermon.",
    '40:3': "The captain of the guard gives the theological account of the war, and gives it accurately.",
    '40:4': "\"All the land is before thee.\" The chains come off and the choice is left wholly to the prisoner.",
    '40:5': "Victuals and a reward from the army that sacked the city. The prophet is provisioned by the enemy.",
    '40:6': "Offered Babylon and offered the open road, he goes to Mizpah, to the people who have nothing left.",
    '40:7': "The captains in the fields hear there is a governor. Government resumes as a rumour reaching armed men.",
    '40:9': "\"Fear not to serve the Chaldeans.\" Gedaliah's entire policy is that submission is now the way to live.",
    '40:10': "Wine, summer fruits and oil gathered into vessels. The sign of the new order is an ordinary harvest.",
    '40:11': "Jews in Moab, Ammon and Edom hear and start back. The remnant is announced by refugees turning round.",
    '40:12': "They gathered wine and summer fruits very much. The best season in the book is the year after the fall.",
    '40:14': "Warned of the plot, with the paymaster named, Gedaliah believed them not.",
    '40:15': "Johanan offers a quiet killing to prevent a killing, and argues it as care for the remnant.",
    '40:16': "\"Thou speakest falsely of Ishmael.\" He defends his assassin's good name, and it costs everyone the land.",
    # ---- Chapter 41 ----
    '41:1': "Of the seed royal, and they did eat bread together. The murder is done at a table by a guest with a claim.",
    '41:2': "Ten men against the one man Babylon had trusted. The remnant's government lasted a few months.",
    '41:3': "The Chaldean garrison is killed too. Whatever Ishmael intended, he has made the province a revolt.",
    '41:4': "\"The second day after he had slain Gedaliah, and no man knew it.\" The country was run by a secret.",
    '41:5': "Fourscore mourners with offerings and incense, walking toward a temple that is no longer standing.",
    '41:6': "He goes out to meet them weeping. The grief he performs is for the man he killed the day before.",
    '41:7': "Pilgrims with incense in their hands, killed inside the town they had been welcomed into.",
    '41:8': "Ten men buy their lives with hidden wheat and honey. The killing stops where there is profit in stopping.",
    '41:9': "The pit was Asa's, dug against an earlier invasion. The old defence work serves as the mass grave.",
    '41:10': "The king's daughters, committed to Gedaliah by Babylon, are marched toward Ammon as property.",
    '41:14': "The captives turn round and walk to Johanan. Ishmael's following evaporates at the sight of soldiers.",
    '41:15': "He escapes with eight men to the Ammonites. The man who ruined the remnant is never accounted for.",
    '41:17': "They camp at Chimham by Bethlehem, already facing Egypt. The retreat is settled before it is asked about.",
    '41:18': "They are afraid of the Chaldeans, and the reason for the fear is a murder they did not commit.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 42 ----
    '42:2': "\"We are left but a few of many.\" The request for guidance opens by pleading how little is left of them.",
    '42:3': "\"The way wherein we may walk.\" They ask for direction with the direction already chosen.",
    '42:4': "\"I will keep nothing back from you.\" He promises the whole answer before he knows what it will be.",
    '42:5': "They name the LORD a true and faithful witness between them, and that is the oath about to be broken.",
    '42:6': "\"Whether it be good, or whether it be evil, we will obey.\" Unconditional, and sworn ten days too early.",
    '42:7': "Ten days of silence. The answer they had sworn to obey did not come on the day they wanted it.",
    '42:10': "Build and plant, not pull down and pluck up: the verbs of his commission at last running the other way.",
    '42:11': "\"Be not afraid of the king of Babylon, of whom ye are afraid.\" The fear is named, then simply forbidden.",
    '42:12': "God undertakes to give Nebuchadnezzar mercy toward them. The conqueror's clemency is treated as supplied.",
    '42:14': "No war, no trumpet, no hunger of bread. Egypt is wanted for the three things Jerusalem could not give.",
    '42:15': "\"If ye wholly set your faces.\" The warning grants that their minds are made up, and speaks anyway.",
    '42:16': "The sword and the famine they fled will follow them there. What is run from is what arrives.",
    '42:18': "The fury poured out on Jerusalem will be poured out in Egypt. Distance from the city is not distance from God.",
    '42:19': "\"Know certainly that I have admonished you this day.\" He goes on record so nobody can plead ignorance.",
    '42:20': "\"Ye dissembled in your hearts.\" The vow to obey was false when it was made, not broken afterwards.",
    '42:21': "The disobedience is dated to the day the answer came. There was no interval in which they weighed it.",
    '42:22': "They will die in the place they desire to go. The sentence is the destination, kept for them.",
    # ---- Chapter 43 ----
    '43:2': "\"Thou speakest falsely.\" Having sworn to obey whatever came, they reply by disputing that it came.",
    '43:3': "The word is blamed on Baruch. Easier to credit the secretary's politics than the LORD's disagreement.",
    '43:4': "\"Obeyed not the voice of the LORD, to dwell in the land of Judah.\" The disobedience was refusing to stay.",
    '43:6': "The prophet and his scribe appear on the list of those taken. He goes to Egypt as baggage, not as a guide.",
    '43:7': "So they came even to Tahpanhes. The exodus is run backwards, and the text names the town without comment.",
    '43:9': "Great stones hidden in the clay of Pharaoh's brickkiln, where nobody will see them until they matter.",
    '43:10': "Nebuchadrezzar is called my servant, and his throne is promised a footing in Egyptian brick.",
    '43:11': "Death, captivity and the sword, each to those appointed for it. Egypt is sorted the way Judah was.",
    '43:12': "He will wear Egypt as a shepherd puts on his garment, and walk out of it in peace, unhurried.",
    '43:13': "The sun-temple images broken. What Egypt still had, and Judah had envied, is taken from Egypt too.",
    # ---- Chapter 44 ----
    '44:2': "\"Ye have seen all the evil.\" The argument begins from what these particular hearers watched happen.",
    '44:3': "Gods whom they knew not, neither they nor their fathers. The idolatry was not even inherited conviction.",
    '44:4': "\"Rising early and sending them.\" The prophets are described as God's persistence, not their own.",
    '44:7': "\"Against your own souls.\" The damage of the sin is charged first to the people committing it.",
    '44:8': "The incense travelled with them. What they carried into Egypt is the thing that emptied Judah.",
    '44:9': "Four generations of wickedness listed, the wives twice. The indictment refuses to be vague about who.",
    '44:10': "\"They are not humbled even unto this day.\" A ruined country has produced no change of mind at all.",
    '44:11': "\"I will set my face against you.\" The face turned toward them in blessing is turned the other way.",
    '44:12': "From the least even unto the greatest. The sentence on the remnant makes no distinction of rank.",
    '44:14': "They have a desire to return to Judah. The desire is granted to so few that the sentence corrects itself.",
    '44:15': "The men knew their wives burned incense. The household arrangement is entered in the record as consent.",
    '44:16': "\"We will not hearken unto thee.\" After all the argument, the refusal is stated with perfect clarity.",
    '44:17': "\"Whatsoever thing goeth forth out of our own mouth.\" They name their own word as the authority.",
    '44:18': "They date the famine from the reform: things went wrong when the queen of heaven stopped getting her cakes.",
    '44:19': "The women point out that the men approved it. The defence is accurate and helps neither party.",
    '44:21': "\"Did not the LORD remember them?\" The incense they thought rose to her was noticed by someone else.",
    '44:22': "\"The LORD could no longer bear.\" Patience is described as having a limit, and the limit was reached.",
    '44:25': "Their vows are quoted approvingly back at them: you said it, you did it, and you will see it through.",
    '44:26': "The punishment is that his name will not be spoken by them again. They lose the formula they abused.",
    '44:27': "\"I will watch over them for evil, and not for good.\" The word for keeping is turned inside out.",
    '44:28': "\"Whose words shall stand, mine, or theirs.\" The whole quarrel is finally about whose speech holds.",
    '44:29': "A sign is given, but the sign is that the punishment arrives, not that it might still be avoided.",
    '44:30': "Pharaohhophra will go the way of Zedekiah. The protector they crossed a desert for gets the same sentence.",
    # ---- Chapter 45 ----
    '45:1': "Four verses of Scripture addressed to a secretary, dated to the year he wrote the roll that was burned.",
    '45:3': "\"I fainted in my sighing, and I find no rest.\" Baruch's complaint is quoted in full before it is answered.",
    '45:4': "The answer to the scribe's grief is the scale of the loss: God is pulling down what he himself built.",
    '45:5': "\"Seekest thou great things for thyself? seek them not.\" He is promised his life and nothing besides.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 46 ----
    '46:2': "Carchemish settled which empire would dispose of Judah. The oracle is dated to that battle's year.",
    '46:3': "\"Order ye the buckler and shield.\" The oracle opens by drilling the army it has come to bury.",
    '46:4': "Furbish the spears, put on the brigandines. The preparations are itemised to make the rout cost more.",
    '46:5': "\"Wherefore have I seen them dismayed?\" The question is asked as though the sight were a surprise.",
    '46:6': "The swift and the mighty are told not to get away. Speed and strength lose their usual exemption.",
    '46:8': "\"I will go up, and will cover the earth.\" The Nile's yearly flood becomes the empire's boast about itself.",
    '46:9': "Ethiopians, Libyans and Lydians handle the shield. Egypt's front rank is made of other nations' men.",
    '46:10': "A sacrifice by the Euphrates. The battle is described as an act of worship with the army as victim.",
    '46:11': "Balm fetched from Gilead, and many medicines that will not close this wound. Egypt is past treatment.",
    '46:12': "\"The mighty man hath stumbled against the mighty.\" In the retreat the strong fall over each other.",
    '46:14': "Migdol, Noph and Tahpanhes are told to stand fast — the very towns Judah's refugees had settled in.",
    '46:15': "\"They stood not, because the LORD did drive them.\" Egypt's collapse is credited to another nation's God.",
    '46:16': "The hired men decide to go back to the land of their nativity. Bought courage has somewhere else to be.",
    '46:17': "\"Pharaoh king of Egypt is but a noise.\" The verdict on the great protector is that he was loud.",
    '46:18': "Tabor and Carmel: the invader is given the permanence of landmarks, sworn to by the King's own life.",
    '46:19': "\"Furnish thyself to go into captivity.\" Packing for deportation is set down as household instruction.",
    '46:20': "A very fair heifer, and destruction coming out of the north. The prize animal is the one that is taken.",
    '46:21': "Her hired men are fatted bullocks. Paid soldiers eat well, and they leave together when the day comes.",
    '46:22': "The axes come as to a wood, and the forest is her army. Egypt is felled rather than fought.",
    '46:25': "Her gods and her kings are punished in one list, and with them all them that trust in Pharaoh.",
    '46:26': "\"And afterward it shall be inhabited, as in the days of old.\" Even Egypt's sentence has an afterward.",
    '46:27': "\"Fear not thou, O my servant Jacob.\" Among oracles against the nations, one nation is called servant.",
    '46:28': "Corrected in measure, and no full end made. The difference between Israel and the nations is the measure.",
    # ---- Chapter 47 ----
    '47:2': "Waters rising out of the north to overflow the land. The invasion is given the image a coast people fear.",
    '47:3': "Fathers will not look back for their children, for feebleness of hands. Panic is measured by what it drops.",
    '47:4': "Tyrus and Zidon lose every helper that remained. The Philistines fall and their allies are counted in.",
    '47:5': "\"How long wilt thou cut thyself?\" The mourning rites are broken into by a question about the mourning.",
    '47:6': "\"O thou sword of the LORD, how long?\" Someone begs the weapon itself to go back into its scabbard.",
    '47:7': "It cannot be quiet because it has a charge. The instrument is not free to stop before the errand ends.",
    # ---- Chapter 48 ----
    '48:1': "Nebo, Kiriathaim, Misgab. The oracle opens on the plateau towns where Israel had once pitched camp.",
    '48:2': "\"There shall be no more praise of Moab.\" The first loss named is reputation, before any wall falls.",
    '48:4': "\"Her little ones have caused a cry to be heard.\" The ruin is measured by the sound children make.",
    '48:5': "Weeping on the road up and a cry of destruction on the road down. The highways are given voices.",
    '48:6': "\"Be like the heath in the wilderness.\" Survival is offered on the terms of a shrub nobody bothers with.",
    '48:7': "Trust in works and treasures, and Chemosh goes into captivity with his own priests. The god is deported.",
    '48:8': "No city shall escape, and the valley and the plain go with them. The geography is named in the sentence.",
    '48:9': "\"Give wings unto Moab.\" Escape would take something Moab has not got, and the verse says so dryly.",
    '48:10': "A curse on doing the LORD's work deceitfully, set next to a curse on sparing the sword.",
    '48:11': "Wine left on its lees, never poured from vessel to vessel. Moab's fault is having been comfortable.",
    '48:12': "Wanderers sent to make him wander, and the jars emptied. The settled wine is finally decanted.",
    '48:13': "Ashamed of Chemosh as Israel was ashamed of Bethel. The comparison flatters neither party in it.",
    '48:14': "\"How say ye, We are mighty and strong men for the war?\" The boast is quoted and left to answer itself.",
    '48:15': "Chosen young men gone down to the slaughter, said by the King whose name is the LORD of hosts.",
    '48:17': "\"How is the strong staff broken, and the beautiful rod!\" The lament is put into the neighbours' mouths.",
    '48:18': "Dibon is told to come down from her glory and sit in thirst. The high seat is traded for the ground.",
    '48:19': "Aroer stands by the road asking refugees what happened. News comes only from the people running.",
    '48:24': "\"All the cities of the land of Moab, far or near.\" The list closes by refusing to leave any out.",
    '48:25': "The horn cut off and the arm broken. Both figures for a nation's power, and both of them bodily.",
    '48:26': "Made drunken, and wallowing in his vomit. The nation that kept its wine is handed a different cup.",
    '48:27': "\"Thou skippedst for joy.\" Moab's sentence rests on how it behaved while watching Israel go down.",
    '48:28': "Moab is told to leave the cities and nest in a cliff like a dove. Safety now means living small.",
    '48:29': "Loftiness, arrogancy, pride and haughtiness stacked into one verse. The repetition is doing the work.",
    '48:30': "\"His lies shall not so effect it.\" The boasting is treated as a plan, and dismissed as one.",
    '48:31': "\"Therefore will I howl for Moab.\" The judge takes up mourning for the nation he is sentencing.",
    '48:32': "The vine of Sibmah reached the sea, and the spoiler has fallen on its summer fruits and its vintage.",
    '48:33': "Wine made to fail in the presses. The treading goes on and the shouting that belongs to it does not come.",
    '48:34': "\"The waters also of Nimrim shall be desolate.\" The springs are counted among the casualties.",
    '48:35': "The high places silenced and the incense stopped. The judgement reaches the worship, not only the walls.",
    '48:36': "\"Mine heart shall sound for Moab like pipes.\" The instrument chosen is the one played at funerals.",
    '48:37': "Bald heads, clipped beards, cut hands, sackcloth. Grief is described purely as what it does to bodies.",
    '48:38': "\"Broken like a vessel wherein is no pleasure.\" A jar nobody wanted is thrown out without regret.",
    '48:40': "An eagle spreading its wings over Moab. The shadow that covers the country belongs to a bird of prey.",
    '48:42': "Destroyed from being a people, because he magnified himself against the LORD. The charge is stated once.",
    '48:43': "Fear, the pit and the snare — three words that chime in Hebrew, laid out as a closing trap.",
    '48:44': "Out of the fear into the pit, out of the pit into the snare. Escape is only movement between traps.",
    '48:45': "Fire comes out of Heshbon, the town they sheltered under. The refuge turns out to be the fuel.",
    '48:46': "\"The people of Chemosh perisheth.\" Moab is named by its god, and the god could not keep its people.",
    '48:47': "\"Yet will I bring again the captivity of Moab in the latter days.\" The oracle leaves Moab a future.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 49 ----
    '49:1': "\"Hath Israel no sons?\" The charge against Ammon is squatting: they moved into an emptied inheritance.",
    '49:2': "\"Israel shall be heir unto them that were his heirs.\" The stolen land is promised back, with theirs.",
    '49:4': "\"Who shall come unto me?\" Full valleys and full treasuries made a nation that could not imagine invasion.",
    '49:5': "Driven out every man right forth, with nobody to gather the stragglers. The flight has no one organising it.",
    '49:6': "Ammon, who took Israel's fields, is promised its captivity brought again. The oracle does not end in ruin.",
    '49:7': "\"Is wisdom no more in Teman?\" Edom's reputation was counsel, and the question is whether any is left.",
    '49:8': "\"Dwell deep.\" The counsel to a people of caves is to go further into the rock, and it will not serve.",
    '49:9': "Grapegatherers leave gleanings and thieves stop when they have enough. Edom is stripped past both.",
    '49:10': "\"I have uncovered his secret places.\" The hiding places were the entire defence, and they are known.",
    '49:11': "Inside Edom's sentence, the orphans and widows are told to trust him, and are promised their lives.",
    '49:12': "Those whose judgment it was not to drink the cup drank it. Edom's plea of desert is answered with Judah's.",
    '49:13': "Sworn by himself, there being nothing higher to swear by. Bozrah's ruin is guaranteed in that manner.",
    '49:14': "An ambassador sent among the heathen to raise the army. Edom's enemies are recruited as messengers.",
    '49:15': "Small among the heathen and despised among men. The punishment is a reduction in standing, and intended.",
    '49:16': "A nest in the clefts of the rock, high as the eagle's, and brought down. The stronghold was the delusion.",
    '49:18': "Sodom and Gomorrah are named as the precedent, so that nobody can argue about the degree of it.",
    '49:19': "\"Who is that shepherd that will stand before me?\" The lion comes up and the questions go unanswered.",
    '49:20': "\"The least of the flock shall draw them out.\" The great nation is dragged off by the smallest thing to hand.",
    '49:21': "The noise of the fall is heard as far as the Red sea. The collapse is measured by how far it carries.",
    '49:23': "Hamath and Arpad faint at the tidings. The oracle on Damascus opens with the neighbours hearing of it.",
    '49:25': "\"The city of praise, the city of my joy.\" The lament for a foreign capital is spoken in the first person.",
    '49:27': "Benhadad's palaces burn. The dynasty that harried Israel for generations is reduced to an address.",
    '49:28': "Kedar and Hazor live in tents. Even people with nothing to besiege are on Nebuchadrezzar's list.",
    '49:29': "Curtains, vessels, camels. The plunder taken from nomads is an inventory of ordinary household goods.",
    '49:30': "\"Nebuchadrezzar hath conceived a purpose against you.\" Desert tribes learn they are inside someone's plan.",
    '49:31': "A wealthy nation dwelling alone, with neither gates nor bars. Having no walls is listed as the opportunity.",
    '49:32': "Scattered into all winds, out of the utmost corners. A people already spread thin is spread further.",
    '49:34': "The Elam oracle is dated to Zedekiah's first year, and reaches past Babylon to the country behind it.",
    '49:35': "\"I will break the bow of Elam, the chief of their might.\" A nation is addressed through its one weapon.",
    '49:36': "Four winds from the four quarters, and no nation the outcasts fail to reach. The scattering is total.",
    '49:38': "\"I will set my throne in Elam.\" A judgement seat is planted in a country that never heard of him.",
    '49:39': "Elam is promised its captivity brought again. Three of these nations are left with a latter day.",
})
